s in the warmup-dominated regime (m ~ p), so simulator-vs-formula agreement is expected only at large m"], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a8a617a495754b22bfb284b0aa61fd86", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-a8a617a495754b22bfb284b0aa61fd86", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"answer": "EXPERIMENT DESIGN \u2014 Throughput vs. Microbatch Count for 1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p (ZB-H1), and ZB-2p (ZB-H2)\n\nDESIGN-ONLY DELIVERABLE. No experiment was executed; no results are claimed. The /workspace repository contains no code (verified: only task.json; track.md states \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\"), so all schedule logic below is grounded in the Zero Bubble Pipeline Parallelism paper (Qi et al., ICLR 2024) and its Megatron-LM fork conventions.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n1. RESEARCH QUESTION AND TESTABLE PREDICTIONS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nQuestion: Does throughput of 1F1B, 1F1B-I, and ZB-1p improve as the number of microbatches m increases, while ZB-2p maintains high throughput even at small m?\n\nHypotheses stated as testable predictions (reference points from analytical formulas, not foregone conclusions):\n- P1 (1F1B): iteration time T = (m + p \u2212 1)(t_f + t_b); bubble fraction (p\u22121)/(m+p\u22121) shrinks with m, so throughput improves monotonically with m and asymptotes at m \u226b p.\n- P2 (1F1B-I, interleaved with V model chunks per rank): same monotone improvement with m; at equal m \u2265 warmup needs, bubble is reduced by roughly a factor of the interleaving depth versus 1F1B, but warmup depth scales with the chunk structure, so 1F1B-I is expected to be MORE sensitive to small m than non-interleaved 1F1B (tested, not assumed).\n- P3 (ZB-1p / ZB-H1): eliminates Type-II bubbles (the backward-dependent portion) but retains warmup-dependent bubbles, whose reference form is \u2248 (p\u22121)\u00b7t_f per rank versus 1F1B's (p\u22121)(t_f+t_b). Prediction: throughput improves with m (bubble fraction \u2248 (p\u22121)t_f / (m(t_f+t_b) + (p\u22121)t_f)) while remaining strictly above 1F1B at equal m; the advantage grows as t_b/t_f grows.\n- P4 (ZB-2p / ZB-H2): near-zero bubble already at m \u2248 p (the paper's central claim), i.e., throughput nearly flat in m across the sweep, at the cost of extra in-flight activation buffers. One sentence note: the paper also defines an interleaved extension ZB-H2-I (ZB-2p-I), which is out of scope as a primary method here.\n- P5 (composite): the qualitative ranking at m = p is ZB-2p > ZB-1p > 1F1B \u2248 1F1B-I-or-worse; at large m the four methods converge.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n2. INSTRUMENT: DETERMINISTIC EVENT-DRIVEN SCHEDULE SIMULATOR\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nA discrete-event simulator (pure Python stdlib, CPU-only) constructs each schedule's timeline from its paper definition:\n- 1F1B: warmup depth p \u2212 rank \u2212 1 forwards before steady-state 1F-1B alternation; cooldown drains remaining backwards.\n- 1F1B-I: V model chunks per rank, Megatron-style warmup formula (per-chunk warmup of (p \u2212 1) scaled by chunk position); forward/backward events alternate across chunks. Constraint honored in the sweep: m must be divisible by V.\n- ZB-1p (ZB-H1): allows a backward to be scheduled before the matching send-forward completes per the paper's dependency relaxation (B passes depend on the corresponding F's output availability for loss, but the send to the next stage is decoupled), eliminating Type-II bubbles; warmup depth p \u2212 1 like 1F1B but with T-type passes allowed in warmup.\n- ZB-2p (ZB-H2): additionally permits double-forward (2F) scheduling enabled by an extra in-flight activation buffer per rank, achieving zero total bubble in the idealized setting. Per the paper's buffer analysis, ZB-H2 requires at most one additional in-flight activation per rank versus ZB-H1; the simulator will enforce the paper's warmup/buffer discipline and record, rather than assume, the minimum m at which the zero-bubble property holds (prediction: m \u2265 p suffices; m < p degenerates as warmup dominates).\nEach event carries (rank, pass type, chunk, start, duration); the scheduler greedily dispatches ready events respecting the dependency rules of that method. Time is discrete on a fine common grid so all methods share the same clock granularity.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n3. METRIC DEFINITIONS (same scale for all four methods)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n(a) Throughput, primary: TP = m / T_iter, i.e., microbatches completed per unit simulated wall-clock time per pipeline (T_iter = end-to-end time of one full iteration = makespan across all ranks). Secondary, scale-free: normalized efficiency \u03b7 = (m\u00b7(t_f+t_b)) / (p\u00b7T_iter) \u2014 work done per rank divided by the ideal p-rank execution time; \u03b7 = 1 means a perfectly filled pipeline.\n(b) Bubble fraction, consistently defined: BF = total_idle / (p\u00b7T_iter) = 1 \u2212 \u03b7, where total_idle = \u03a3_rank (T_iter \u2212 busy_time_rank) and busy_time counts only forward/backward compute (communication excluded per the simplifying assumption below). BF and \u03b7 are algebraically linked, giving one coherent scale across 1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p, ZB-2p.\nAlso logged per cell: per-rank idle histograms (to locate warmup/cooldown vs. mid-iteration bubbles), max in-flight activations per rank (ZB-H2 buffer cost), and, for ZB-2p, the smallest m at which BF \u2264 1% is achieved.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n4. VARIABLES AND SWEEP (including small m)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nManipulated: method \u2208 {1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p, ZB-2p}; m \u2208 {\u2308p/2\u2309 (degenerate probe), p, 2p, 4p, 8p} \u2014 the sweep deliberately includes m at and below the practical minimum so ZB-2p's small-m behavior is directly tested, not extrapolated.\nControlled: p \u2208 {4, 8, 16}; t_f:t_b ratio \u2208 {1:1, 1:2, 1:4} (t_f = 1.0 time unit normalized; t_b = 2\u00b7t_f as the headline configuration, matching the paper's typical backward \u2248 2\u00d7 forward); interleaving depth V = 4 chunks per rank for 1F1B-I (m restricted to multiples of V; this constraint is reported when it bites, e.g., m = p with p = 8 gives only 2 microbatches per chunk); communication = zero for all methods (simplifying assumption, resolved by the researcher); identical per-microbatch FLOPs across methods.\nGrid size: 4 methods \u00d7 3 p \u00d7 5 m \u00d7 3 ratios = 180 simulator runs (with divisibility exclusions for 1F1B-I), each run milliseconds \u2014 a few seconds total on system Python.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n5. FAIR-COMPARISON PROTOCOL\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n- Identical per-microbatch compute cost (t_f, t_b) and identical stage count p across methods in every comparison.\n- When throughput is compared across methods at different m, comparisons are made (i) at equal m (pure schedule-quality comparison) and (ii) at equal global batch via a secondary scaling analysis (throughput per token: TP \u00d7 microbatch token size, fixed). Headline claims use (i); (ii) shows whether ZB-2p's flat curve translates into practical small-batch wins.\n- Warmup policy fixed per method exactly as defined in the paper; no ad-hoc tuning per cell.\n- ZB-2p's extra buffer memory is reported as an overhead column but excluded from timing (memory does not enter the simulated clock).\n- Identical event-grid granularity and zero-communication treatment for all methods.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n6. SENSITIVITY ANALYSIS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nAcross t_f:t_b \u2208 {1:1, 1:2, 1:4} and p \u2208 {4, 8, 16}, report a qualitative-conclusion matrix: which predictions (P1\u2013P5) hold in every cell and which are ratio- or depth-dependent. Expected outcomes to be tested: (i) P4 (ZB-2p near-zero bubble at m \u2248 p) should be robust to all settings since it is warmup-structure-driven; (ii) P3's ZB-1p-over-1F1B gap should widen with t_b/t_f because the eliminated Type-II bubble is proportional to t_b; (iii) at t_f:t_b = 1:1 the ZB-1p advantage shrinks toward parity with 1F1B, which is a genuine finding if observed, not a failure; (iv) 1F1B-I's small-m degradation (deeper warmup) should be visible at p = 16, V = 4. The design explicitly treats any non-robust conclusions as reportable results.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n7. VALIDATION PLAN\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n- V1 (1F1B exactness): assert simulated T_iter = (m + p \u2212 1)(t_f + t_b) and BF = (p\u22121)/(m+p\u22121) at large m (m \u2265 4p) for all p and ratios; any deviation indicates a scheduler bug.\n- V2 (1F1B-I sanity): at large m, simulated BF should approach (p\u22121)(t_f+t_b)/(V\u00b7m\u00b7(t_f+t_b) + (p\u22121)(t_f+t_b)) \u2014 i.e., roughly a V-fold reduction versus 1F1B at equal m \u2014 and per-rank idle should be concentrated at the warmup head and cooldown tail with no mid-iteration gaps.\n- V3 (ZB-1p): simulated BF at large m should match the reference (p\u22121)t_f/(m(t_f+t_b)+(p\u22121)t_f) within the time-grid tolerance.\n- V4 (ZB-2p): BF \u2264 1% for m \u2265 p across all ratio/p cells; log the observed minimum-m threshold against the paper's buffer analysis rather than assuming it.\n- V5 (cross-check): qualitative trend comparison against the paper's reported bubble/throughput figures (1F1B, interleaved, ZB-H1, ZB-H2 curves).\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n8. CONTROLS, THREATS TO VALIDITY\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nControls: fixed per-microbatch cost across methods; equal global batch in the secondary comparison; paper-defined warmup policies; identical clock grid; zero and identical communication; p and ratio swept jointly for both methods in every pairwise comparison.\nThreats to validity (stated, not hidden): (1) idealized zero communication and no overlap modeling \u2014 real 1F1B-I pays more communication per microbatch than non-interleaved, so real-world 1F1B-I may look worse than simulated; (2) no memory-bandwidth or kernel-launch effects; uniform, straggler-free stage costs; (3) ZB-H2's exact warmup/buffer discipline at small m is reconstructed from the paper's description and the fork's conventions \u2014 the V4 check mitigates but cannot eliminate this; (4) analytic reference formulas are approximations for small m (warmup-dominated regime), so simulator-vs-formula discrepancies at m \u2248 p are themselves informative; (5) results are for the idealized single-pipeline model, not any specific model architecture (though the appendix maps parameters onto a real model's measured t_f/t_b).\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n9. ANALYSIS PLAN\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nPrimary output: a table and plot of TP (and BF) versus m for each method at the headline configuration (p = 8, t_f:t_b = 1:2), with the sensitivity matrix as a robustness appendix. Decision rules: P1\u2013P3 supported if TP is strictly increasing in m with diminishing returns and the ZB-1p > 1F1B gap holds at equal m for every cell; P4 supported if ZB-2p's TP at m = p is within 5% of its TP at m = 8p (near-flat curve) while 1F1B's TP at m = p is materially below its asymptote. All comparisons report the sim-clock tolerance used.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n10. FEASIBILITY STATEMENT\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nScope: each schedule simulation produces O(p\u00b7m\u00b72) events for non-interleaved methods (\u2264 16\u00b7128\u00b72 \u2248 4k events) and O(p\u00b7m\u00b72\u00b7V) worst case for interleaved (\u2248 16k events); an event-queue + ready-list dispatcher over integer time slots handles this in milliseconds. Fully deterministic (fixed dependency rules, no randomness) \u2014 no seeds or repeats needed; variance is zero by construction. Total grid of ~180 runs runs in seconds on CPU-only system Python with zero third-party dependencies (heapq, csv, json from stdlib only). All feasibility claims are estimates of the instrument, not executed runs.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nAPPENDIX (optional, brief): mapping onto a Megatron-fork deployment\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nOn a real cluster, p maps to --pipeline-model-parallel-size, m to --num-micro-batches (global batch = m \u00d7 microbatch size \u00d7 data-parallel size), 1F1B-I to --num-layers-per-virtual-pipeline-stage > 1, and the zero-bubble schedules to the fork's zero-bubble scheduling toggles (exact flag names in srvttg/zero-bubble-pipeline-parallelism could not be verified against a local repository, which is absent). t_f and t_b would be measured per-stage per-microbatch from real forward/backward timings; the simulator's zero-communication assumption corresponds to high-bandwidth intra-node interconnect, and the BF column would be validated against torch profiler idle gaps.", "assumptions": ["No real GPU cluster is used: throughput and bubble fraction come from the deterministic simulator, with the paper's published trends as the external cross-check", "Schedule logic for 1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p (ZB-H1), and ZB-2p (ZB-H2) is reconstructed from the Zero Bubble Pipeline Parallelism paper and Megatron-fork conventions since no repository code exists in /workspace", "Communication is zero and identical across methods, and no communication/computation overlap is modeled (researcher-resolved simplifying assumption)", "ZB-2p refers to ZB-H2 only; ZB-H2-I is mentioned in one sentence but excluded as a primary method (researcher resolution)", "Per-microbatch forward/backward costs are fixed constants identical across methods; backward is set to 2x forward in the headline configuration"], "evidence": "Repository observations: ls -la /workspace shows only task.json (651 bytes); task.json's documents reference track.md stating \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\"; a filesystem-wide find for zero-bubble/pipeline-parallel content returned nothing; environment is network-disabled, GPU-less, system Python without third-party packages. Design requirements incorporated per researcher resolutions: (a) explicit metric definitions TP = m/T_iter, eta = m(t_f+t_b)/(p*T_iter), BF = 1 - eta = total_idle/(p*T_iter) on one shared scale; (b) microbatch sweep m in {ceil(p/2), p, 2p, 4p, 8p} covering and going below the practical minimum, with ZB-H2's minimum-m constraint from the paper's buffer analysis treated as an observed simulator output (V4) rather than an assumption; (c) hypothesis section restated as testable predictions P1-P5, with ZB-1p framed as eliminating Type-II bubbles only (remaining warmup bubble ~ (p-1)*t_f, so throughput still improves with m but stays above 1F1B at equal m) and ZB-2p framed as predicted near-zero bubble at m ~ p; (d) sensitivity grid t_f:t_b in {1:1, 1:2, 1:4} x p in {4, 8, 16} with a qualitative-conclusion matrix; (e) validation plan V1-V5 including the 1F1B analytical identity T_iter = (m+p-1)(t_f+t_b), BF = (p-1)/(m+p-1) and a chunked 1F1B-I sanity check (~V-fold bubble reduction, idle concentrated at schedule head/tail); (f) feasibility estimate: O(p*m*2) to O(p*m*2*V) events per run (~4k-16k), deterministic, no seeds, milliseconds per run, ~180 runs in seconds on stdlib-only CPU Python; (g) controls (identical per-microbatch cost, equal global batch secondary comparison, paper-defined warmup policies, common clock grid) and explicit threats (no comm overlap, no memory-bandwidth effects, ZB-H2 warmup/buffer detail at small m, analytic-formula approximation at small m). Optional appendix maps parameters onto Megatron-fork flags with flag names marked unverifiable against the absent repository.", "stage": "result", "summary": "Final experiment design document delivered for researcher review", "uncertainties": ["Exact warmup/buffer discipline of ZB-H2 at small m is reconstructed from the paper; the simulator will measure the minimum m for the zero-bubble property rather than assume it", "Exact Megatron-fork flag names cannot be verified against the absent repository; the deployment appendix uses generic descriptions", "Whether the interleaving depth V = 4 matches the researcher's intended real-world configuration; the sweep grid can be regenerated for other V", "Analytical reference formulas are approximations in the warmup-dominated regime (m ~ p), so simulator-vs-formula agreement is expected only at large m"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-0add6c4655f24671bb107e7a0bb92858", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Final experiment design document delivered for researcher review", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
